---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import {estrofasDeCanto} from '../../../../lib/corpus';
const selections=[1145,1147,1157];
const sections=[{"heading":"Learning through hardship is not infallibility","body":"Fierro acknowledges the possibility of error. He does not erase his earlier actions by turning them into lessons. This is a useful distinction when reading a narrator who has been mistreated and has also harmed others."},{"heading":"Knowing when anger begins","body":"His concern with recognizing when someone becomes angry invites attention to self-control and conflict. The line has force because the earlier poem includes encounters in which passions and violence produce irreversible consequences."},{"heading":"Astuteness, prudence and opportunism","body":"The same resourcefulness may become prudence or cunning at another person's expense. The poem does not reduce survival to innocence or villainy; it raises questions about how skills are used."},{"heading":"A contemporary reading","body":"To learn from mistakes is not simply to repeat a moral phrase. It can mean acknowledging injury done to others, accepting responsibility and changing conduct. This application is our commentary, clearly separate from Hernández’s original language."}];
const stanzas=estrofasDeCanto('vuelta',32).filter(s=>selections.includes(s.numero));
---
<Base lang="en-US" adKind="article" pageType="Article" title="Learning from Mistakes in Martín Fierro: Wisdom Born of a Hard Life" description="Explore Martín Fierro's advice on learning from mistakes, experience and prudence, with original Canto XXXII verses and English analysis.">
<article class="study"><nav class="crumb"><a href="/en/">English home</a> / <a href="/en/library/">Popular library</a> / <a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">Advice to his sons</a></nav><header><p class="eyebrow">FATHERHOOD · FAMILY · WISDOM · ORIGINAL SPANISH VERSES</p><h1>Learning from Mistakes in Martín Fierro: Wisdom Born of a Hard Life</h1><p class="intro">Fierro says he never attended a school other than an unfortunate life. His reflections on knowledge, anger and prudence are especially striking because readers know he has not always followed the standards he recommends.</p></header><figure><img src="/media/fotografias-v3/consejos-fogon-hijos.webp" alt="Modern illustrated reconstruction of Martín Fierro giving counsel to three young men around a campfire"/><figcaption>Contemporary editorial illustration; not an archival document.</figcaption></figure><div class="reading"><section><h2>The original verses, Canto XXXII (1879)</h2><p>These selected stanzas are drawn directly from the site's original Spanish corpus; editorial explanation follows separately.</p>{stanzas.map(s=><section class="stanza" lang="es"><h3>Estrofa {s.numero}</h3><blockquote>{s.versos.map(v=><span>{v.texto}</span>)}</blockquote><a href={`/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/#${s.id}`} lang="en">See the stanza in the complete canto</a></section>)}</section><AdSlot id="article-02-after-original-verses" kind="article"/><section><h2>Reading the poem with care</h2>{sections.map((s,i)=><><section><h3>{s.heading}</h3><p>{s.body}</p></section>{i===1&&<AdSlot id="article-03-mid-commentary" kind="article"/>}{i===2&&<AdSlot id="article-04-late-commentary" kind="article"/>}</>)}</section><section class="sources"><h2>Original text and reading companions</h2><p>José Hernández, <em>La vuelta de Martín Fierro</em> (1879), Canto XXXII. The quotations are read from the same repository corpus as the Spanish reader. This article is original English interpretation, not a translation or a claim about Hernández's personal beliefs.</p><p><a href="/leer/vuelta/32/" lang="es">Read the complete original canto</a> · <a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">All 31 stanzas with commentary</a> · <a href="/en/library/themes-and-analysis/">Literary analysis</a></p></section><AdSlot id="article-05-end" kind="article"/></div></article>
